  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nPeriodic \nprecipitation chances continue to be the main forecast challenge in \nthe long term. Upper level low pressure will track from the Pacific \nNW into the northern Rockies Sunday into Sunday night. In advance of \nthis feature, a lead shortwave will approach the central Rockies \nSunday afternoon. As this feature interacts with a narrow band of \nfavorable thetae and moisture along the front ranges of Colorado and \nWyoming, thunderstorms are expected to develop in these areas late \nSunday afternoon into the early evening hours. Southwesterly winds \nwill carry this activity east into western Nebraska Sunday evening. \nA low level jet, oriented from Kansas north into the Nebraska \nSandhills, will help to sustain convection into the overnight. ATTM \nbased on the latest deterministic GFS and NAM12 solns, along with \nthe NBM ensemble forecast, areas across the Sandhills into north \ncentral Nebraska stand to see the best chances for precipitation \nSunday night into Monday morning. In the wake of precipitation \nMonday, upper level low pressure will track across southern \nSaskatchewan into southern Manitoba. Deepening surface low pressure \nin association with the upper level low, will force a cold front \ninto the western Dakotas Monday. This feature will approach \nnorthwestern Nebraska Monday afternoon, pushing through the \nremainder of the forecast area by Monday evening. In advance of this \nfeature, very warm air ahead of the front will lead to highs in the \n80s with near 90 in the southwest. In a setup similar to the last \n12 hours, westerly winds in advance of the frontal boundary, will \nlimit low level moisture Monday, focusing the threat for \nprecipitation INVOF the front Monday evening. That being said, the \nbest chances for precipitation Monday night into Tuesday will be \nalong the cold front. This favors the southern half of the forecast \narea. As for severe chances, low level moisture remains meager \nMonday-even in the southeastern forecast area. Limited CAPE is noted \nMonday afternoon/evening in the southeast. Given the meager amount \nof CAPE, believe the severe threat will be limited at best. \nOvernight Monday night, the front will be forced south into Kansas \nwhere it will stall for the next 24 to 36 hours. This will limit \nprecipitation chances Tuesday into Tuesday night across the area. By \nWednesday, a deepening trough of low pressure aloft over the \nintermountain west, will begin to draw the front north bringing \nbetter precipitation chances to the southern half of the forecast \narea Wednesday into Wednesday night. These chances will continue \nThursday into Friday as the upper level trough across the \nintermountain west, slowly transitions east northeast into the \nnorthern Rockies.",
